         new SelfTest("conversation_resume", Severity.Light, "Player report (fkasad, 20/09/2026): roleplay takes longer than a battle, so he quits mid-negotiation, saves, closes the game, comes back, and the lord remembers TALKING to him but not a word of what was said, so the bargain starts over. Drives the REAL store the chat uses (NpcMemoryCampaignBehavior.ConvResume): keeps a built transcript against a real lord, then asserts it comes back whole for the same lord on the same day with its memory anchor, that the SAME open cannot take it twice, and that it is refused and DISCARDED for another lord, another day, or a staged scene, which is the report's own 'deleted as soon as you open a new chat' rule. No LLM call, no window opened. Forgets whatever it kept afterwards", TestConversationResume),
         new SelfTest("approach_framing", Severity.Light, "Player report (Fakade, 19/09/2026, local model): a lord rode out to him, then from the next turn spoke as though the player had come to HIM. The opening directive is consumed on read, so who sought whom was in the prompt once and never again. Stashes a real fief-audience reason on a lord, builds the encounter context twice through the REAL builder, and asserts the directive fires on the first build and is spent on the second while NpcSoughtThePlayer stays true on both; then asserts opening a new conversation (ForgetConversationSeeker) clears it. Leaves no pursuit behind", TestApproachFraming),
         new SelfTest("realm_departure", Severity.Light, "Player report (tashmetu, 19/09/2026): expelled from his kingdom by another mod after a lost war, he kept getting letters as if he still belonged. Records a departure from a real kingdom the way the kingdom-change feeder now marks every one, and asserts WorldEventFeeders.DaysSincePlayerLeft finds it and silences that ruler's service offer (ServiceOfferPolicy.RulerMayWrite), then removes it and asserts the ruler may write again. Also asserts the service-offer premise follows the player's LIVE realm. No LLM call, mutates nothing it does not remove", TestRealmDeparture),
         new SelfTest("interception_nemesis_bounty", Severity.Light, "A third proactive channel, the announcement/discoverability layer on top of the EXISTING scripted hero_main_options nemesis bounty offer (roadmap piece 2, player wish, subsumes the 'tavern rumour: Coll raids near X' wish since the lord tells the player directly): a lord whose region is plagued by a nemesis he is eligible to bounty seeks the player out to name him and where he operates, never settling the bounty itself in this channel. Mints a nemesis anchored to a REAL lord's own region (MintBountyEligibleNemesis) and asserts InterceptionBehavior.HasNemesisBountyToOffer reuses the SAME NemesisBountyDialogFlow.FindEligibleNemesisFor gate the scripted offer uses (never a re-derived one), that the Detail names the nemesis, that the pure InterceptionPolicy.Choose surfaces NemesisBounty, that it is fief-valid, and the priority pin (a live grudge against the PLAYER still outranks it, mirroring InterceptionPolicyTests). Marks the minted identity dead afterwards", TestInterceptionNemesisBounty),
         new SelfTest("letters_mailbox", Severity.Light, "Mechanical: asking to stop writing (via the real preference parser + apply seam) drives a sender's correspondence damper to the floor and LOWERS that sender's mailbox weight; an Acknowledge also lowers it; the weighted scheduler prefers the important letter; a full budget window releases nothing; a 40-day-old event candidate is stale", TestLettersMailbox),
         new SelfTest("conversation_cut", Severity.Light, "Player report (17/09/2026): characters recited the conversation back, replies growing every turn; and (raphareish, 18/09/2026) asked after the progress of a quest agreed thirty seconds earlier. Both cures are a COUNT taken when the conversation opens, and both counts are produced by the HOST (ChatViewModel) and honoured by the SDK, so the pure tests on either side cannot see the wire between them. This is the wire: seeds one memory and one task on a real profile, takes the two cuts through the REAL ChatViewModel, seeds a second memory and task to stand for what the exchange itself writes, asserts neither cut moves, then builds the EXACT prompt the chat pipeline builds (LlmServices.ChatService.PromptBuilder, via EncounterContextBuilder.Build) and asserts the character still holds their past, does not have this conversation replayed at them as history, and reads a task agreed at the table as JUST AGREED rather than an errand under way. No LLM: the whole question is what the prompt CONTAINS, and an answer from a model would be a weaker reading of the same fact. Removes everything it seeded", TestConversationCut),
         new SelfTest("letter_memory", Severity.Light, "Player report (Desporion): a marriage proposal 'agreed on the terms' over three letters was denied face to face because letters left only a generic 'Received a letter (Reason)' memory, never their substance. Pure half: LetterMemoryPolicy.ShouldRemember excludes the two re-ping reasons (AwaitingReply, QuestUpdate) and includes MarriageProposal; BaseMemory is first-person and reflects direction. Real-path half: drives the REAL LetterMemoryFinalizer.RecordLetterMemory (LetterPopupManager.MarkReceived's own seam) with a ShouldRemember=false reason (QuestUpdate), so the run can never fall through to the async LLM summarizer (Lane C, kept out of cr.test all), and asserts the base event actually lands on profile.Events. Removes the event it added", TestLetterMemory),
         new SelfTest("world_conditions", Severity.Light, "Mechanical: the engine reads the weekly world-conditions feeder depends on still work against the live game (a renamed field or a changed model signature would silently swallow to 0 inside the feeder's own guards, recording nothing forever). Reads GetScoreOfDeclaringWar in both directions between two live kingdoms and a live town's FoodStocks/FoodChange/Prosperity DIRECTLY, asserts each is finite, then runs the real RunScan and asserts both scanners ran. Skips when the world has fewer than two kingdoms or no town", TestWorldConditions),
         new SelfTest("realm_tidings", Severity.Light, "Mechanical: drives the REAL RealmTidingsBehavior.DebugEvaluate end to end and proves per-NPC dedup. Seeds a dominant known event for a friendly landed lord (regard forced to 30), asserts a RealmTidings letter candidate is queued and the event id enters the dedup ledger, then re-evaluates and asserts the same event is never relayed twice. Restores the writer's regard, ledger and candidates and removes the seed", TestRealmTidings),
         new SelfTest("siege_tidings_participation", Severity.Light, "Mechanical (player report: told by letter of a siege they FOUGHT IN, days later, as if it were news): stamps the REAL WorldEventFeeders siege-participation marker for a live settlement, then drives the REAL RecordSiegeCapture construction with a NON-player taker (a mere participant, never the credited capturer) -> assert the recorded WorldEvent has InvolvesPlayer true (so realm-tidings' own InvolvesPlayer filter will never relay it back) while Type stays SettlementCaptured and Aggrieved* stay empty (the conquest's attribution is NOT stolen from the real taker). Removes the seeded event afterwards", TestSiegeTidingsParticipation),
         new SelfTest("companion_skirmish_memory", Severity.Light, "Thoragoros1 (Nexus, 15/10/2026): companions remembered none of the early fights (bandits, a patrol, lords' lone parties), because only chronicle-worthy battles left a memory. Arranges a companion in the party with NO profile yet (hired through the game's own dialogue, never spoken to here), drives the real small-fight recorder twice, and asserts a profile is made and keeps ONE memory counting both fights and naming the foe. Puts the companion's own profile back exactly", TestCompanionSkirmishMemory),
         new SelfTest("companion_battle_memory", Severity.Light, "Mechanical: the companion 'fought together' memory (WorldEventFeeders' main-party companion loop), which a real MapEvent drives in game. Borrows a companion into the party if none rides along, calls the DebugRecordCompanionBattleMemories seam, and asserts the companion's profile gained a fresh 'Fought at your side' memory, then that a second one carries what each side lost (3chmidt, 05/11/2026). Removes the memories it added and returns any borrowed companion", TestCompanionBattleMemory),
         new SelfTest("witness_standing", Severity.Light, "Who the people in the room are, and the speaker's own family in Compact (fearente, Nexus, 29/10/2026: a lady governor spoke to her father, the head of her clan, like a servant; a ruler was treated like an ordinary noble; a brother spoke of a son 'Lorgand' when he has a daughter). Drives the REAL ChatViewModel with a real character and a real living parent brought in as a companion of the player: asserts the parent's line, as the child will read it this turn, names them 'your father' or 'your mother' and, when they lead the child's clan, 'head of your clan'; that the ruler of a realm is named its ruler to a lord of another realm; that the parent's family list closes its children ('and no other children'); and that the Compact prompt, built by the real PromptBuilder, keeps the family (YOUR FAMILY) while RELATIONSHIPS is dropped", TestWitnessStanding),
         new SelfTest("reply_length", Severity.Light, "The MCM Reply Length (RaveN017Mod, Nexus PM, 29/10/2026: replies too long, 'like a novel'). Sets the REAL MCM dropdown to Short, asserts the live chat service is rebuilt with it (no restart) and that the real prompt for a real character teaches 1 to 3 sentences and not the shipped 3-7; then restores the player's choice", TestReplyLength),
         new SelfTest("participants_panel", Severity.Light, "The chat's participants panel (RaveN017Mod, Nexus PM, 29/10/2026: invited clan members 'often don't interact much at all', and he wanted a way to make clear who he addresses; Gabriel's answer the same day: a panel listing the active voices and the witnesses, a click on a witness makes them active). Drives the REAL ChatViewModel with a real lord and three real witnesses brought in through AddWitness: asserts the panel is offered and lists all four, that a click on a witness turns the player to them and draws them in, that the third voice fills the table and a fourth is answered but stays a witness (three at most: each voice is a model call every turn), that Step back sends a voice back and returns the player to the lord when it was the one addressed, and that an emptied room (a private audience) takes the departed out of the cast so the scene is no longer an ensemble", TestParticipantsPanel),
         new SelfTest("spotlight_cast", Severity.Light, "Spotlight pillar wiring (found in play by Gabriel 2026-09-12: he addressed his companion Sley by name in a conversation with Ira, the turn routed to Sley, and no [SPOTLIGHT] line was ever written - the promotion silently did nothing). ConversationCast has been correct and unit-tested since 27/08; both faults were in the HOST. (1) The cast was born inside SetWitnesses, so a conversation with no auto-detected witness - a lord standing alone - had no cast at all, not even its own anchor. (2) AddWitness never introduced the arrival, and AddWitness is how a companion joins: the picker resolves ASYNCHRONOUSLY, after the cast was built, so every brought companion sat in the witness list while absent from the cast, Promote read RoleOf == null and returned false. ConversationCast.Introduce was written for exactly this on 27/08 and had never once been called from the game. Drives the REAL ChatViewModel: sets a real anchor hero with NO witnesses and asserts a cast exists with that anchor Active (hole 1), then AddWitness a real companion and asserts they are IN the cast as a Witness and therefore promotable (hole 2), then promotes and asserts the scene became an ensemble. A pure test cannot catch either: it would build the very cast the host failed to fill", TestSpotlightCast),
         new SelfTest("private_memory", Severity.Light, "The leak guard the companion briefing is built on (Gabriel 2026-09-13: what was decided in a briefing should live in the companion MEMORY and be used from there). A memory is normally shared: a witness carries theirs into the prompt of whoever is speaking, which is right for \"he was there when you took the town\" and catastrophic for \"Arwa asked me to charm Ira\", read out in Ira own prompt while she stands there. Until now the briefing dealt with that by DROPPING its memories outright, so nothing of the private word survived. NotableEvent.IsPrivate now marks a memory the holder keeps to themselves. Plants one PUBLIC and one PRIVATE event on a real hero profile and drives the REAL ChatViewModel.DebugWitnessMemory - the same BuildWitnessMemory path a live turn uses, and the ONLY path by which one hero memories reach another hero prompt - asserting the public one is carried and the private one is not, and that a hero holding NOTHING but private memories contributes no recall at all rather than an empty husk. Restores the profile events in a finally", TestPrivateMemory),
         new SelfTest("briefing_to_chat", Severity.Light, "The WHOLE briefing-to-meeting vector, end to end, driven through the real objects (Gabriel asked for it 2026-09-13 after three faults in one evening that every existing test was green through - each piece worked and the joins did not). Borrows two companions and a third hero as the target. Drives: RememberReturn (what the picker decides, without the picker), a REAL ChatViewModel put into briefing mode with both companions seated, four REAL player turns recorded into its transcript, then the REAL FinalizeCompanionBriefs. Asserts the private word reached the memory of BOTH companions and not just the anchor; that it is marked private; that the FIRST thing said survives as well as the last (the cap that ate his mission statement); that the target cannot read it through the REAL witness-recall path NOR through a REAL EncounterContextBuilder.Build with both companions standing as witnesses (the load-bearing property); that the return is armed at the right target with both companions; and that the REAL staged request names them so the meeting reopens with them seated. What it CANNOT prove is the two moments only play can: that the window opens and that the tick fires. Restores both profiles and clears the pending return in a finally", TestBriefingToChat),
         new SelfTest("companion_briefing", Severity.Light, "Companion-briefing pillar as it stands after the standing brief was dismantled (13/09/2026): the private word becomes a PRIVATE MEMORY on everyone who stood in it, and that memory never reaches the person it was about. Borrows a companion into the party if none rides along; picks a second, distinct living hero to stand in as the conversation's TARGET. Plants a real IsPrivate NotableEvent and drives the REAL EncounterContextBuilder.Build (no LLM call) for both heroes: asserts the companion's own prompt CARRIES it (they must act on what they were told) while it is ABSENT from the target's - the load-bearing property of the whole pillar, since BuildWitnessMemory is the single path by which one hero's memories reach another's prompt and it filters IsPrivate. Also asserts a NON-private event on the same companion DOES reach the target, so the test cannot pass by the memory simply never travelling. Removes the planted events in a finally and returns the borrowed companion", TestCompanionBriefing),
         new SelfTest("query_relevant_facts", Severity.Light, "Modder report: asking a lord a POINTED question about a specific third party got a hallucinated answer because the real fact ranked too low on ambient awareness (the small WorldRumorsBlock top-N) to ever be injected. Seeds a fresh, high-magnitude, faction-tagged WorldEvent naming a real third-party lord (guaranteed KNOWN regardless of map position), then drives the REAL EncounterContextBuilder.ResolveQueryRelevantFacts (no LLM call) once with a message naming that lord -> assert the seeded fact surfaces, and once with an unrelated message -> assert it does NOT (the knowledge-then-relevance gate, never omniscience). Removes the seeded event afterwards", TestQueryRelevantFacts),
         new SelfTest("war_council_offer", Severity.Light, "Mechanical: the war-council OFFER trigger's arm + policy + persisted-cooldown decision (WarCouncilOfferBehavior.DebugWouldOffer), WITHOUT opening the council UI. Arms the flag and asserts a notable action while leading an army WOULD offer, that not-in-army / non-notable / disarmed all refuse, and that stamping an offer today blocks a fresh one (cooldown) while a stale stamp clears it. Restores the flag + the persisted last-offer day", TestWarCouncilOffer),
         new SelfTest("interception_holds_course", Severity.Light, "Lordfadooboo (Nexus, 30/09/2026): a lord announced as riding out to meet the player passed him by or rode into a city. Steers a real free lord party onto the player as a pursuit does and checks it is held on course (its own AI may not take the order back), then ends the steering and checks the party is its own again and told to think afresh. Refuses while a real pursuit runs.", TestInterceptionHoldsCourse),
         new SelfTest("party_release", Severity.Light, "Mechanical: the STARVING LORD bug (Fakade). Freeze a real lord party exactly as the old stand-down did (SetMoveModeHold, which is an ORDER to stand still, not a release), prove it looks stranded, then prove BOTH halves of the fix: releasing hands it back to its own AI (RethinkAtNextHourlyTick), and the on-load rescue frees a party already stranded by an earlier version", TestPartyRelease),
         new SelfTest("retainer_spike", Severity.Heavy, "FEASIBILITY SPIKE: take a real OTHER-CLAN, partyless lord/lady into MobileParty.MainParty via AddHeroToPartyAction, assert (a) they are in the main member roster and (b) their Hero.Clan is UNCHANGED (still their own, NOT the player clan) with occupation still Lord; drive one RunDailyObservation (this behavior's daily handler) and assert they SURVIVED it at peace (reports honestly - this drives only the mod's own daily seam, not vanilla's full party-spawn tick); assert the pure RetainerLeavePolicy forces a leave on war; then release and assert they left the roster and are partyless again. Restores all borrowed state; skips MANUAL when no partyless other-clan lord exists (a party-leading lord's own party would be disbanded, which is not cleanly reversible in a live campaign)", TestRetainerSpike),
         new SelfTest("ride_with_me", Severity.Heavy, "the PLAYER-FACING ride_with_me / part_ways path through the REAL bridge (not the raw mechanism the retainer_spike test drives): (e) while RetainerBehavior.Enabled is OFF the verb REFUSES and takes no one; (a) enabled, an idle at-peace other-clan lord ends up in MainParty.MemberRoster with Hero.Clan UNCHANGED (not the player clan); (b) a party-LEADING lord is REFUSED (that is follow_me's job) with their own party intact, proving the two verbs are mutually exclusive on the leads-a-party fact; (c) part_ways removes them (out of the roster, partyless, untracked); (d) the pure RetainerLeavePolicy forces a leave on war; (f) Lordfadooboo/Maifailun (Nexus, 01-03/11/2026): taken again, a liege leading an army (forced through RetainerBehavior.TestCalledToMuster, since a real army cannot be raised and undone) releases them on the daily pass. Restores regard + the feature switch + any paused war (GABRIEL'S RULE, 13/10/2026: made peace via the real MakePeaceAction when no candidate was already at peace with everyone, re-declared after) in a finally; skips MANUAL only when no partyless other-clan lord exists at all to borrow", TestRideWithMe),
         new SelfTest("stolen_love", Severity.Light, "Stolen Love pillar (non-destructive): on a real third-party-married NPC, assert the seducibility pipeline runs on live facts (SeductionFactsMapper -> SeductionEligibilityPolicy) without throwing and yields a bounded verdict; drive the estrangement state machine via the debug seam (seed -> None in the opening grace -> declare -> ReadyToDivorce) and assert NO divorce has fired (HasConcludedFor false); assert the jilted-spouse grudge magnitude for the real couple sits within [Min,Max]. Clears the seeded estrangement in a finally so NO marriage is ever touched. Skips when no third-party-married NPC exists", TestStolenLove),
         new SelfTest("reasoning_scrub", Severity.Light, "Mechanical: the inline <think> leak (tthheerroocckk, nemotron on High reasoning). The save/load itself is STUBBED: corrupted memories are written straight into the store as an old save would carry them (a trace before real prose, a memory that is ONLY a truncated trace, a corrupted background paragraph) -> the REAL session-launch scrub runs -> assert the prose is recovered, the all-trace memory becomes the honest 'faded' line (never invented content), the background returns to null, and a second pass is a no-op. Removes its seeds afterwards", TestReasoningScrub),
         new SelfTest("duel_consequence", Severity.Light, "Mechanical: the DUEL consequence glue (DuelOutcomeApplier), which no unit test can reach (it writes to the live campaign). Seed a courted lady's AttractionToPlayer to 0, then drive the REAL applier for a CourtshipRivalry duel: WIN raises her attraction by exactly CourtshipDuelPolicy.AdmirationOnWin, a following REFUSAL drops it by CowardiceOnRefuse (proving the forfeit path and that refusing costs MORE than losing), each duel records a world-event so she can learn of it, and a win lifts the player's renown. Restores her attraction afterwards", TestDuelConsequence),
         new SelfTest("menu", Severity.Light, "Mechanical: the in-chat Options button reaches the vanilla escape menu purely by REFLECTION, so a TaleWorlds rename would not throw, it would just go quiet while the log claimed success (exactly the bug SuperGraham339 reported across three versions). Asserts BOTH mechanisms the game answers ESC with still resolve: the MISSION view (a settlement, a conversation, a naval scene) and the map's navigation handler. Whichever one is missing is a screen the player's menu will never appear on", TestMenu),
         new SelfTest("panel_siege_guard", Severity.Light, "Mechanical: the full-map panel crash guard. Asserts the Harmony prefix that skips SettlementVisualManager.OnTick (the vanilla siege-bombardment visual tick, which runs on the frame loop and access-violates natively under a pushed CR panel: four confirmed 0xC0000005 crashes) is actually attached to the LIVE engine method. It resolves the target BY NAME, so a game update that renamed the type/method would silently stop protecting; this fires if that ever happens. Also asserts that with no panel open nothing is suppressed (siege visuals tick normally). No LLM, mutates nothing", TestPanelSiegeVisualGuard),
         new SelfTest("spawn_agent_signature", Severity.Light, "tashmetu (Nexus, 25/10/2026): Mission.SpawnAgent(AgentBuildData, bool) is gone in the 1.5.5 beta (two optional parameters added), so a duel or a captive's escape compiled against 1.4.8 would throw MissingMethodException as its first fighter spawns. CR now finds the overload at run time. Asserts the running game offers one CR can call: public, on the mission, AgentBuildData first, every other parameter optional. Read-only, spawns nothing", TestSpawnAgentSignature),
         new SelfTest("setting_hints_fit", Severity.Light, "Mechanical: every MCM hint fits on screen. A shipped bug with a receipt - v2.5.6 repaired NINETEEN hints that ran off the edge, and because they read \"On (default): ... Off: ...\" the clipped half was always the one explaining the other position. They were fixed by hand with nothing left to stop the twentieth, which 2026-09-12 then added. Reads the ATTRIBUTES, so it holds whether or not MCM has loaded. Read-only", TestSettingHintsFit),
         new SelfTest("mcm_test_buttons_wired", Severity.Light, "Mechanical: the three MCM Test buttons (OpenRouter, OpenAI-Compatible endpoint, NanoGPT) reach the connection tester. Since 19/10/2026 the settings no longer name the tester (it reads them, and the two namespaces used each other): the submodule hands it over at load, so a button whose hook was never set would click and do nothing, with no error. Asserts each hook is set. Read-only, no call is made", TestMcmTestButtonsWired),
         new SelfTest("memory_panel_secrets", Severity.Light, "tashmetu (20/10/2026): a player read his wife's secret affair in her lover's Memories panel before it came to light. Gives a met character, through the REAL public door, a private memory from another mod, an open one from another mod, and a private one under CR's own name (the player's word to a companion), and asks the REAL panel rule: with Show secret memories off, only the other mod's secret is left out; on, it is listed marked secret. Restores the setting and removes the three memories", TestMemoryPanelSecrets),
         new SelfTest("tale_arc", Severity.Light, "Gabriel (25/10/2026), on the night in Rhagaea's chamber: 'ca pourrait etre trop court ou trop long', then an arc of beats that 'devrait diriger l'experience que le LLM fait vivre au joueur'. A tale written into this test (a talk that completes as the conversation closes, with an arc of two beats of 2 and 1 exchanges) is opened through the REAL OPEN_TALE path with a lord. The lord's REAL prompt carries the first beat, not the second; after two exchanges counted by the same call the chat makes, the second beat is in the prompt and told it is the LAST reply; the third exchange closes the arc (the chat would close softly) and not before; the close then completes the stage on its ending. Then the signals (Gabriel: 'signaux observes avec tirage en filet'): a second tale whose middle beat goes on only if a signal was seen; through the REAL bridge (tale_signal) a signal the arc does not watch is refused, the watched one is taken and sets its flag, and the arc then skips back nowhere and reaches its last beat; played again with nothing seen and a net roll forced to succeed, the game decides the signal and the lord's prompt tells her to narrate it. The tales are forgotten after.", TestTaleArc),
         new SelfTest("tale_interrupted", Severity.Light, "Gabriel (25/10/2026): 'je fais la quete du loup, mais je suis fait prisonnier par Rhagaea.. je devrais pouvoir vivre la nuit avec elle et reprendre ensuite la quete du loup lorsque je suis libere de prison', with the clock stopped while it waits and one level only. Two tales written into this test, both opened through the REAL OPEN_TALE path with a lord: the second sets the first aside (the first waits, the second plays); the daily journal reconciling leaves the first's entry open; a third summons is refused while one waits; the second ended, the first stays aside while the player is held, and is taken up again once free, at its stage, its first day moved on by the days it waited. The tales are forgotten after.", TestTaleInterrupted),
         new SelfTest("tale_opened_by_scenario", Severity.Light, "Gabriel (24/10/2026): a scenario opens a tale at night while the player is held (ON_CAPTIVE_NIGHT, IF_CAPTOR_IS, IF_PRESENT, IF_CHANCE, OPEN_TALE); the night is judged by the regard it left ('ca s'est bien passe si Rhagaea a gagne plus de Regards qu'elle en a perdu'), the memory stays either way, and each playing shows a new video of the stage's pool. A tale written into this test (on_scenario, a talk that completes as the conversation ends, an image_pool, three memories under regard_rose / regard_fell and a regard reward) is opened through the REAL OPEN_TALE check with a lord; the talk does not complete as the chat opens, and the lord is told the chamber's moment while it lasts; the lord's regard is raised by 4 during it; the close completes it with regard_rose, the warm memory and the +5 reward; a video of the pool is chosen. Then the same tale refused at its popup (decline_ending) ends at once: -2 regard and its memory, and, being the tale's second playing with that lord, it carries what the first left (played_before, ended_before:<the first ending>: Gabriel, 26/10/2026, 'que Rhagaea se souvienne des autres fois'). IF_CHANCE:1 holds and IF_CHANCE:0 does not; IF_CAPTOR_IS fails while the player is free. A lord with an errand merely on offer (Gabriel, 25/10/2026: Rhagaea's night refused with 'they already have a task of their own for you') can still be summoned, when the world holds one. Regard, memories, pools and the tale are put back after.", TestTaleOpenedByScenario),
         new SelfTest("tale_keys", Severity.Light, "tashmetu (20/10/2026), four keys for written tales: requires.player.gender, a notable giver's trade, a town as the bound place, gold the player pays. A tale written into this test asks a merchant of a town and the player's own sex: a real town merchant binds, with the town as its place; a headman or a village notable is refused for the trade; its twin asking the other sex is refused for the player. One gold paid by the player through the REAL payment reaches the merchant, then is given back. Forgets the tales after", TestTaleKeys),
         new SelfTest("edict_effects", Severity.Light, "Gabriel (21/10/2026), edicts on towns and realms, by the player and by the world's lords, and contestable. All six channels must be attached to the live models (tashmetu, 25/10/2026: a mod model inheriting CalculateTownTax left the tax unattached). Picks a real town held by a lord and has its holder decree a Heavy levy through the REAL decree (EdictBehavior.Decree): the influence is spent, the game's OWN loyalty model now carries a line named for the edict at -1 a day and its tax model a +25% factor (what the town's tooltip shows). A second edict on the same town and a decree by someone who does not hold it are refused before anything moves. A notable of the town is told the edict in his own prompt. Then the town is made restless with a long grievance and one day runs: its people force the edict off and the loyalty line is gone. Restores the town's loyalty, the holder's influence, and removes the edict and its news", TestEdictEffects),
         new SelfTest("lord_strategy", Severity.Light, "Gabriel (29-30/10/2026): lords play their realm's strategy, plotting against a house grown too strong (the player's too) and building a counterweight against a realm grown too strong through the game's own war, peace, alliance and call to arms. Reads the live balance (the dominant realm, or the strongest as a stand-in when the balance holds) and prepares, WITHOUT filing, each other realm's step through the REAL path (LordStrategyBehavior.Prepare): the player's own realm is never stepped for, a prepared step is one the game allows, filed by a house that is not the player's and can pay its influence, its news names the realm, and nothing has been filed. Then finds a house head with a house grown too strong over his own (SchemeStore.SelectOvershadowingTargetFor): the target heads a house at least twice as strong, of his own realm or one at war with it, is neither his kin nor a ruler. The prepared step's realm vote is weighed through the game's own KingdomElection, never started. Changes nothing", TestLordStrategy),
         new SelfTest("strategist_aim", Severity.Light, "Gabriel (30/10/2026), second increment of the strategist aims: a lord turns to the player for help against the realm grown too strong, and asks only for a deed the game will verify (gating before declaration). Picks a free house head who holds no aim, whose realm is at war with a realm that is not the player's, and gives him the REAL aim (StrategistAims.Compose, under its own source tag): it is voiced in his conversation context with leave to ask outright for the service, its deed heads his grounded quest menu as attack_faction on that realm (the token and name the quest factory binds), and the weekly pruning keeps it while the war holds. Then the same lord asks a raid on one of that realm's villages: raid_village on that village heads his menu and his want names it. Retracts the aim", TestStrategistAim),
         new SelfTest("enact_edict", Severity.Light, "Gabriel (21/10/2026): the player decrees edicts for his towns and realm. With a town of the player's (none: skipped, since arranging one is a real settlement transfer that cannot be cleanly undone) and a member of his house to carry it, the verb is taught with the catalogue; a worded decree of Feast days named 'the Test Feast' is asked first, the question naming its effect, term and influence; then the REAL bridge decrees it for the player and the town's own loyalty model names it. An edict outside the catalogue is refused. Removes the edict and its news, restores the influence", TestEnactEdict),
         new SelfTest("player_fame", Severity.Light, "Gabriel (21/10/2026), after reading another mod's notoriety per culture: a character who never met the player is told how far the player's name has reached them. Picks a real foreign lord who never met the player and builds his REAL encounter context (EncounterContextBuilder.Build, what a conversation sends): with the player's renown at 0 he is not told the name is famous, at 1000 he is told he would know the banner, and the line names the player's character. A member of the player's own clan gets no line. Restores the player clan's renown", TestPlayerFame),
         new SelfTest("tale_items", Severity.Light, "tashmetu (20/10/2026), items in tales: \"bring 30 grain to {giver}\" and a thing given in an ending; Gabriel approved it for a second phase. A tale written into this test (never a shipped file) is taken on with a real lord and asks him 3 grain; the party is given 8. The bag sees the goods asked of him (and of nobody else), a bag hand-over of 2 takes nothing and leaves the stage; a give_item worded in the chat (Gabriel 21/10: it counts) through the REAL bridge takes nothing while the party carries 2, is asked first naming the 3 asked, then takes exactly 3 of the 8, ends the tale, and its ending gives 2 wine through the REAL item consequence. Puts the party's grain and wine back, abandons and forgets the tale", TestTaleItems),
         new SelfTest("tale_locals_memory", Severity.Light, "tashmetu (20/10/2026), the Moonlit Maiden: a giver who is a notable of the village an ending speaks to remembered the same moment twice, as the giver and as one of the locals. Binds a real notable of a real village as the giver of an ending that gives him a memory and speaks to the village's locals, and asks the REAL recipients: the giver is left out of the locals' memory, and the village's other notables still hear it. Writes no memory", TestTaleLocalsMemory),
         new SelfTest("settings_ranges_agree", Severity.Light, "Mechanical (Gabriel, 2026-09-10, after the Regard Warmth Cooldown: \"je suis surpris que le bon comportement n'ait pas ete bien protege par des tests unitaires. Par exemple, 0 aurait du etre teste\"). The uncomfortable truth is that 0 WAS tested and the test agreed with the bug: it pinned what the code did, while the MCM control clamped its minimum to 1 and the v2.5.1 changelog promised 0 turned the cooldown off. Three artefacts, no place for any two of them to meet. This is that place. Reflects over EVERY [SettingPropertyInteger] on CrSettings, pairs it with the same-named ModSettings property that enforces it, drives the declared MIN and MAX through the live setting, and asserts the code honours every value the menu offers. Catches the whole family in one assertion, including settings that do not exist yet. Restores each setting to its prior value in a finally", TestSettingsRangesAgree),
         new SelfTest("console_help_complete", Severity.Light, "Mechanical: cr.help against the REAL command registry. Asked on 2026-09-10 whether it was still complete, it described 70 of 259 registered commands, so cr.testall and cr.test were themselves invisible, and it advertised cr.compose, renamed cr.letter_compose long before, which made the one name a reader was most likely to copy verbatim the one that would fail. A hand-written list of every command in a project that gains commands weekly can only drift, so what EXISTS now comes from reflection over the assembly its own [CommandLineArgumentFunction] attributes and the prose is an overlay on top of it. Reads the live registry, drives the real ModCheats.Help in all three of its forms, and asserts: the help never names a command that does not exist (the cr.compose class of error), every registered command is reachable through cr.help all however undescribed, the curated page says where the rest of them are, and the search finds a command by fragment. Read-only, mutates nothing", TestConsoleHelpComplete),
         new SelfTest("seasonal_chronicle", Severity.Light, "The seasonal historian (Gabriel's design): at each turn of the season every realm is read, and what MOVED - a stronghold taken, coffers grown, armies thinned - is written into the world as ordinary news. Computed deterministically from the almanac's own power facts rather than written by a model, because \"Vlandia grew rich\" IS a delta description and a background call every season would cost tokens forever, fail halfway, and have a language to get wrong. Drives the REAL behaviour against the REAL store with a perturbed reading (the honest alternative is waiting a season and hoping), and asserts it is ordinary news rather than a fourth memory store. Removes the row it writes", TestSeasonalChronicle),
         new SelfTest("world_claims", Severity.Light, "Invented facts (Gabriel, 2026-09-12): a character makes up a blight, the gate judges it, and if it passes it becomes a real low-weight rumour a DIFFERENT character can have heard - which is the incoherence Gabriel named, closed through the rumour road that already existed instead of a second world model. A unit test can prove the RULE and never that the row reached the world, so this drives the real injector against the real store. Also asserts the two refusals that matter: the PLAYER never authors the world, and nothing the engine keeps on a screen is ever invented into it. Removes every row it writes", TestWorldClaims),
         new SelfTest("knowledge_composition", Severity.Light, "Mechanical (Gabriel + fkasad, 2026-09-11): the half a unit test CANNOT do. A unit test builds its own EncounterContext, so it can prove a companion WOULD carry his house's standing and never that anyone supplied it - which is exactly where the governor bug lived (the prompt was right, the rule would have been right, the host never filled the field). Sweeps up to 60 free REAL heroes PLUS up to 15 PRISONERS, asks NpcKnowledgeFactory which knowledge packs each one CARRIES, and asserts the live context actually SUPPLIED every one of them; reports the pack census so a pack that reaches nobody is visible rather than silently vacuous. Prisoners joined the sample on 16/09/2026: excluding them left the whole IsCutOff composition unverified in a live game - the one branch where packs are meant to go SILENT, and so the one where a mistake is hardest to spot by reading a transcript - and that blind spot was covering a real defect, since realm_news and underworld ignored the flag and a man in a cell was reading his realm's current fortunes. Read-only: builds contexts, mutates nothing", TestKnowledgeComposition),
         new SelfTest("unavailable_deeds", Severity.Light, "Mechanical (Gabriel's ruling 09/09/2026: \"il faudrait que le LLM declare en connaissant deja le gating\"): a verb used to have TWO states in the prompt, taught in full or ABSENT, and absence does not read to a language model as forbidden, it reads as not mentioned, so the model supplied the permission itself and the player met the contradiction one deed later as a system message refusing what the NPC had just promised. Verbs now state their own closed door BEFORE the model speaks (IStatesUnavailability, the inward twin of the IProvidesGuidance channel built for modders on 30/08/2026 and never turned inward). Sweeps up to 60 real living foreign lords, driving the LIVE VerbRegistry.ComposeUnavailability and ComposeTeachings against each, and asserts: no refusal ever leaks a verb id or an [ACTION] block (which would teach the model the very token it must not emit), and no verb is ever taught and refused in the same prompt. Then takes the first lord the world supplied who has the means but not the trust (arranging one via a REAL ChangeRelationAction when none already stands that way, GABRIEL'S RULE 13/10/2026, restored after) and drives the REAL EncounterContextBuilder.Build, asserting the block reaches the live EncounterContext.UnavailableDeeds and forbids the PROMISE, not merely the deed (a deferred promise being the same failure). MANUAL only when no party-leading lord with a healthy enough force exists at all to arrange", TestUnavailableDeeds),
         new SelfTest("chat_focus_arbiter", Severity.Light, "Mechanical (tashmetu, 2026-09-09): the chat overlay slid off screen on NPC-initiated conversations and never came back, killing every later conversation until a restart. The old arbiter decided \"a screen is above us\" by COUNTING the host screen's layers against the count taken when the chat opened, and vanilla's own MissionGauntletConversationView adds a layer to that SAME screen while the conversation starts: click an NPC and it is already counted, be approached BY one and it arrives after, so the count grows and the chat yields to the very window it exists to replace, forever, because that layer stays for the whole conversation. The rule is now the engine's own IsFocusLayer, with the two vanilla conversation windows named and exempt. Builds REAL GauntletLayers on the live top screen exactly as vanilla builds them (\"MissionConversation\" order 49, \"MissionEscapeMenu\" order 50, IsFocusLayer set, no input restrictions taken) and asserts: ScreenLayer.Name really hands back the name the layer was built with (the one assumption a pure test cannot check), the vanilla conversation layer changes the verdict by nothing at all, a real modal still takes the screen and is NAMED in the log, and removing it reclaims the screen. EXTENDED after Gabriel's play-test (2026-09-09) proved a name list cannot be complete: he opened a chat in a TAVERN, where vanilla's settlement menu \"MapMenuOverlay\" holds focus for the whole conversation, and the chat yielded to the very backdrop it had been drawn over, never appeared, and let the vanilla dialogue run on underneath. Whatever already claims focus when the chat OPENS is scenery, whatever it is called; only what arrives afterwards can be a screen above. Now also builds a real \"MapMenuOverlay\" layer and asserts it is held through when recorded as backdrop, yields when it is not (the exact bug), and that a genuine modal opening OVER that backdrop still takes the screen. Removes every layer in a finally, so a failed assertion can never leave one on the player's screen. No LLM, mutates nothing", TestChatFocusArbiter),
         new SelfTest("panel_state_hosting", Severity.Light, "Mechanical: the panel-hosting migration (PushScreen -> PushState). cr.tick_probe measured a pushed SCREEN leaving MapState ticking at exactly the same rate as nothing pushed at all (481 settlement-visual + 481 map-input ticks per 4s window), while a pushed STATE silenced both to zero, because GameStateManager deactivates MapState before the new screen even exists. Drives a REAL open/close cycle through LetterWindowManager.OpenCompose/Close (the shared CrPanelHost, defaulted to the state strategy) on a borrowed lord's profile, and asserts: the live GameState becomes CrPanelState while open, CrPanelHost.AnyPanelOwnsScreen (the tick-suppression flag every guard reads) engages then fully clears, and the state stack returns to EXACTLY the state that was active before, never left substituted or stuck. Force-closes the window in a finally even if an assertion throws, so a broken assertion can never leave a panel open or the map muted. No LLM", TestPanelStateHosting),
         new SelfTest("dialogue_entry_trace", Severity.Light, "Mechanical (missing-CR-option player reports): DialogueEntryTraceCollector resolves ConversationManager.ActiveToken to a name via reflection on the private stateMap field. Forward-resolves 'hero_main_options' (a token that certainly exists, registered by LlmDialogFlow) to its live index, then reverse-resolves that same index back, driving the real reflection path both ways rather than a parallel copy; asserts the round-trip returns the original name, so a future game update renaming stateMap is caught here instead of silently degrading the diagnostic to raw indices. Also asserts DialogueEntryTracePolicy renders the expected YES/NO verdict for an offered and a not-offered trace. Read-only: touches no live conversation state, nothing to restore", TestDialogueEntryTrace),
         new SelfTest("political_map", Severity.Light, "Mechanical: the political-map gather + grid + rasterise (World Almanac minimap, Inc B+C). Gathers one anchor per kingdom-held settlement (position + Kingdom.Color) from the live campaign, builds the pure PoliticalMapPolicy colour grid, and rasterises it to PNG; asserts the grid is the requested size, some cells are painted (territory), and the PNG carries a real PNG signature. Catches a renamed settlement/terrain API that would silently yield an empty map, or a broken rasteriser. MANUAL when the world has no kingdom-held settlement. No LLM, mutates nothing", TestPoliticalMap),
         new SelfTest("strategic_assessment", Severity.Light, "Mechanical: the Strategic Assessment gather (World Almanac). Builds the live PowerIndexResult, picks the advisor (highest-Tactics companion, else a warband soldier, else none when the player is alone), gathers the player's realm/rank/wars, and composes the OFFLINE factual summary; asserts the summary is non-empty and, when an advisor exists, the LLM prompt grounds on a real kingdom and forbids invention. The real async LLM counsel is Lane C (real LLM), not run here. No LLM, mutates nothing", TestStrategicAssessment),
         new SelfTest("provider_routing", Severity.Light, "Mechanical (per-role provider): set Prose->OpenRouter, Consensual->NanoGPT, Actions Interpreter->OpenAI-Compatible (+ a dummy endpoint) and assert the three resolved base URLs are DISTINCT and each kind matches, via LlmServices.DebugDescribeRoleRouting (no network call); then assert a per-role OpenAI-Compatible endpoint override sends each role to its OWN server while a blank override inherits the shared one; then set every role to (default) and assert all four roles collapse to the ONE default provider's base URL (the migration guarantee: an upgraded single-provider config is unchanged). Snapshots and restores every dropdown + the shared and per-role custom endpoint URLs", TestProviderRouting),
         new SelfTest("background_hold", Severity.Light, "fkasad (Nexus, 15/10/2026): on a local server, summaries, memory compaction and letters slipped in between two replies, the server swapped its KV cache and replies timed out. With a conversation forced open through the seam: a background call routed to OpenAI-Compatible (local) waits; the same call routed to a cloud provider goes at once; with no conversation open a local call goes at once. Restores the provider dropdowns and the seam (the held wait ends within a second)", TestBackgroundHold),
         new SelfTest("war_duty_ledger", Severity.Light, "Maifailun (Nexus, 15/10/2026): a vassal who sits out the realm's war is called to account, so the mod must see his service. On a fresh ledger through the real upkeep: the realm the player is sworn to (none as a mercenary or a free lord) and its enemy kingdoms are recorded from today, so no one owes on the first day. A battle against a realm at war with his counts as service; one against bandits does not. Restores the player's own ledger", TestWarDutyLedger),
         new SelfTest("aims_conversation", Severity.Light, "CrAims (tashmetu, 14/10/2026: lords with aims of their own; 'the real risk is a lord who brings up Lakonon in every conversation'). A test aim is registered on a real lord not at war with the player; a conversation opening with him gives him leave to raise it and the encounter context carries his want; a second conversation the same day does not give it again; the leave ends with the conversation. An aim Calradia Remembers seeds for its own mechanics is not voiced by the generic section. Retracts both aims", TestAimsConversation),
         new SelfTest("initiative_director", Severity.Light, "Gabriel 28/10: one conductor decides whose turn it is to come to the player unasked; its queue and its turns are kept in the save. Writes a copy of the live state with a request and a turn, reads it back through the save's own serializer, and expects the same keys, families, ranks and hours (the init-only fields filled under this runtime). Then asks the rule on that copy at a calm moment and expects a turn given. Touches no live state", TestInitiativeDirector),
         new SelfTest("initiative_dials", Severity.Light, "The older pace dials under the world's initiative (29/10/2026; before, their own rates applied first and the director paced the rest, so the two multiplied). Flips the REAL MCM dropdowns: with the director pacing, the lords' daily roll steps aside (100%) and Lords Approaching You reads as the lords' weight; Never stops them (weight 0, no roll); with the director Off, the shipped roll is back. Restores the player's choices", TestInitiativeDials),
         new SelfTest("initiative_turns", Severity.Light, "Gabriel 28/10: lords and the rest ask the conductor before they come to the player unasked. Through the REAL MayKnock: a lord asking right after another turn waits (too soon), his request is kept, no turn is recorded for him; asked again at a free moment he has it. Skipped when the pace is Off (each system paces itself). The live queue and turns are put back as they were", TestInitiativeTurns),
         new SelfTest("aims_channels", Severity.Light, "CrAims audience and letter channels (tashmetu 14/10: 'nobody takes the first step with the player'; Gabriel 28/10: 'fait 2 et 1'). A test aim open to audiences and letters is registered on a lord at peace with the player: he may raise it by audience and by letter; an audience he sought gives him leave in the conversation that follows, recorded as an audience, even with the conversation channel closed; after it, the letter waits (raised too recently). An aim closed to audiences is not a reason to seek the player. Retracts after", TestAimsChannels),
         new SelfTest("aims_door", Severity.Light, "The public CrAims door (tashmetu, 14/10/2026: 'nobody takes the first step with the player'; proposed in ROADMAP, approved by Gabriel 20/10/2026). Registers an aim for a lord at peace with the player through the REAL door: accepted, updated under the same id, refused for a second aim on the same lord, an unknown hero, and CR's own name; a conversation opened with him gives him leave and the context carries the want; CrAims.Raised tells the mod, with the aim's id, the lord and the channel; retracted, it is gone. Retracts and closes after", TestAimsDoor),
         new SelfTest("player_named_on_screen", Severity.Light, "Gabriel (17/10/2026): a grievance read 'over The marriage was ended by the player's own choice'; never 'the player' in the game, the character by name. Passes a stored cause saying 'the player' through the real grievance line and the real chat message line, and asserts both show the player's name, never 'the player', and that a sentence cause follows a colon. Changes nothing", TestPlayerNamedOnScreen),
         new SelfTest("reply_shape", Severity.Light, "avalanche24 (Nexus, 03/11/2026): a local reasoning model was cut off mid-thought at the fixed 1200-token Compact budget, and the fixed 0.5 presence penalty cut its thinking short. Moves Reply Token Budget and Presence Penalty and asserts the live chat service sends them and keeps the budget in Compact; on Auto, 3000 and Compact's own budget again; restores both settings", TestReplyShape),
         new SelfTest("chat_temperature", Severity.Light, "fkasad (Nexus, 17/10/2026) asked whether the mod tunes the temperature; the conversation value was fixed at 0.7, and Gabriel asked for a setting. Moves the Chat Temperature setting and asserts the live chat service sends the new value, and that an out-of-range value is held in range; restores the setting", TestChatTemperature),
         new SelfTest("companion_patrol", Severity.Light, "jaserms1 (Nexus, 16/10/2026): a council's patrol order did nothing; Gabriel's rulings 16-17/10 (the player's own men, chosen on the game's troop screen, until told to stop) and his spike at Ebereth (a free AI patrol drifts off its post). Arranges a companion and men, confirms a 5-man patrol of the nearest village through the same handler the troop screen calls, and asserts the men really left the main party, the patrol is anchored on the village with the AI free; then drags it away and runs the hourly leash, which must re-anchor it; then (Aporo, Discord 10/11/2026) moves it far off on its way home and asserts it keeps riding before ReturnHomePolicy.GraceDays and rejoins once they are out; then asserts every man is back. tashmetu (18/10/2026): another mod reads the patrol through CrPatrols.Current and hears its end through CrPatrols.Ended, as Back. Cleans up", TestCompanionPatrol),
         new SelfTest("enemy_meeting_offer", Severity.Light, "fkasad (Nexus, 16/10/2026): an enemy lord rode up as the player was about to attack him, the chat opened by force and a local model reasoned for half a minute over an opener nobody read; Gabriel the same day: a popup for hostile lords only. On real lords: one at peace with the player, holding a grudge, still opens the meeting at once; the same lord defending his land asks first; a lord of a realm at war with the player asks first whatever his reason (when such a lord exists). Changes nothing", TestEnemyMeetingOffer),
         new SelfTest("enemy_no_border_challenge", Severity.Light, "LapitaPolata (Nexus, 18/10/2026): lords of a realm at war with the player rode up to ask why they crossed the border with their troops, the war making it plain, and did so outnumbered, when the game has an outnumbered lord flee. On real lords' parties and their live strengths against the player's: a weaker party reads outnumbered and its lord never rides out, even over a grudge; a stronger one is not, never challenges the player's passage at war, still presses a grudge, and a realm at peace still challenges. A missing war is set on the facts. Changes nothing", TestEnemyNoBorderChallenge),
         new SelfTest("war_duty_audience", Severity.Light, "Gabriel's ladder (15/10/2026): the summoned vassal is called in by the ruler himself, at his hall (never on the road: Gabriel, 16/10/2026), and no longer only by letter. With the switch on for the test, a reckoning is opened for a real ruler (one standing in a town when there is one); the duty names him as the one calling the player in; the REAL fief-audience scan of his town picks him with the war duty reason although he has a field party; his opener holds the vassal to the war and leaves the amounts to the terms; once the player has stood before him he no longer sends for them. No letter is written. Restores the ledger, the switch, the audience cooldown and the ruler's memories", TestWarDutyAudience),
         new SelfTest("war_duty_levy", Severity.Light, "Gabriel's ladder (15/10/2026): the levy is paid face to face in coin, horses, weapons or soldiers, and is no gift. With the switch on for the test, a reckoning is opened for a real ruler leading a party and answered in person; his conversation's terms name what is owed; then the REAL bridge (the path an LLM emission takes) runs take_gold, give_item with a horse, and give_troops with recruits arranged into the player's party, and each counts toward the levy until it is settled. No letter is written. Restores the player's and the ruler's gold, the soldiers, the ledger, the switch and the ruler's memories", TestWarDutyLevy),
         new SelfTest("war_duty_reckoning", Severity.Light, "Maifailun (Nexus, 15/10/2026) and Gabriel's ladder: a summons, a levy in coin or goods, a fief. With the switch on for the test and the letters not written (no model call), a reckoning is opened for a real ruler of a realm: he holds the war duty's aim in CrAims; a conversation with him moves it to the levy demanded in person and he remembers it; two hand-overs that meet the levy settle it, he remembers that, and his aim is withdrawn. A second reckoning, never answered, past its deadline brings the levy by letter; riding to war settles it. Restores the ledger, the switch and the ruler's memories; no fief is ever touched", TestWarDutyReckoning),
         new SelfTest("prose_fallback", Severity.Light, "Mechanical (Prose + Interpreter truncation safety net): drives the pure TruncationFallbackPolicy for every clean/not-clean finish reason (length/content_filter/empty -> fallback, stop -> no fallback, no model configured -> no fallback), then asserts LlmServices.ResolveProseFallbackModel resolves the Phase 2 NSFW model, reusing the PROSE model when the NSFW field is blank and honouring a distinct configured NSFW model instead. Then the refused-beat fallback (04/10/2026): the NSFW model resolves for its own provider, the NSFW role has its own client, a refusal is redone there in Enhanced mode, and nothing is redone with no NSFW model. Restores the NSFW model field", TestProseFallback),
         new SelfTest("manual_model_toggle", Severity.Light, "Three-state in-chat switch (Enhanced/Roleplay/NSFW, replaces the old binary manual toggle): drives the pure ModelModePolicy mapping each state to its (main role, composition) pair and the fixed Cycle order (wraps NSFW -> Enhanced), then the live LlmServices.ActiveModelId/ActiveComposition: the NSFW state resolves the configured NSFW model with no MCM gate to pass (the switch is always live now), Roleplay/Enhanced resolve the PROSE model (Roleplay never auto-routes to NSFW for a captivity scene), and only Enhanced yields Enhanced composition. Snapshots and restores the NSFW model field and the active mode", TestManualModelToggle),
         new SelfTest("authored_quest_opening", Severity.Light, "Authored quests Inc 1 (docs/design/AUTHORED_QUESTS.md). The werewolf loads; a real lord holding a village by a forest is bound to it and told to raise it; accepting puts a 'Tale:' entry in the journal and moves past the request; a local of that village believes in the beast and knows nothing of the slavers; two locals heard moves the tale to the watch. Then the watch ends in the scene: a declared finding recorded, an undeclared one refused, the snare hurts but never kills (health restored), closing early still yields the missed clue and moves on to the lord; the lord sends the player hunting, a real band is spawned near the village, and beaten by the player the tale turns home. Every tale binds {player} to the player's name (tashmetu, 11/10/2026). Stops before the reward; abandons the tale (the band is released) and forgets it after. Before the band is beaten, the player loses to it twice through the real after-battle handling (on_defeat, Gabriel 10/10/2026): the tale stays at the hunt and the lord remembers the defeat once.", TestAuthoredQuestOpening),
         new SelfTest("tale_quiet_village", Severity.Light, "fkasad (Nexus, 18/10/2026): the Drowned Bridge was set in a burned village whose town was besieged and fell, its people out of reach, and the tale asked him to retake the town. A raided, looted or besieged village reads as not quiet (when the world has one); a real lord is offered the Drowned Bridge about a quiet village; accepted, it goes on while his house holds the village, and when the village is arranged to belong to another house the tale ends and its journal entry closes. Abandons the tale and forgets it", TestTaleQuietVillage),
         new SelfTest("authored_quest_branches", Severity.Light, "Branching tales (docs/design/quest-editor/goal.md). A small tale written into this test (never a shipped file) loads through the real loader; a real lord is bound to it and it is taken on. At the crossroads the journal shows the stage and its open ways, not the closed one; meeting the lord takes the first OPEN way (the one under an unset flag is passed over). In the scene, the chat would list only the open ways; a marker for a closed way changes nothing; a finding sets its flag, the way it opened is listed and its marker is taken; closing the scene follows it to its own ending (no regard, gold or memory, so nothing is left behind). Abandons and forgets the tale after.", TestAuthoredQuestBranches),
         new SelfTest("authored_quest_said_choice", Severity.Light, "Branching tales, choices by words. A tale written into this test loads and a real lord takes it on. At its last stage the action interpreter of a conversation with that lord is taught the open choice by words and not the one closed by its flag; another lord's interpreter is taught nothing. The REAL prompt of that lord carries the open choice below the CURRENT ENCOUNTER marker on a Standard turn (the character's own call reads the tags, Gabriel 25/10/2026: Enhanced reproduces Standard) and not on an Enhanced one (the character is not told the ways ahead). Through the REAL bridge (story_choice, the path an LLM report takes): the closed choice is refused and changes nothing, the open one is taken and ends the tale on its own ending (no regard, gold or memory). Abandons and forgets the tale after.", TestAuthoredQuestSaidChoice),
         new SelfTest("audience_not_spent_unheard", Severity.Light, "EnjoyingHydra (Nexus, 03/10/2026): companion audiences accepted and never opened were spent anyway (grievance count reset, topics used), and a companion left. Promises a change for a real companion, sends the audience through the real open with no profile so it fails: nothing may be spent, nothing left waiting. Opens no window.", TestAudienceNotSpentUnheard),
         new SelfTest("shared_past_draw", Severity.Light, "Gabriel's rulings on the generated shared past (NPC_REGARD.md section 7, 17/10/2026): drawn once and never re-rolled, written into the world's long-term memory before the campaign began, both adults then, a good share get nothing. Picks lords never drawn with a lord of their realm, drives the real draw (SharedPastBehavior.DrawFor) for up to six pairs: each is marked drawn; each past drawn is in the long-term memory, dated before the start, with both of age then; a second draw changes nothing. Forgets the pairs and their pasts after.", TestSharedPastDraw),
         new SelfTest("letter_from_another_mod", Severity.Light, "tashmetu (18/10/2026): R&S's tournament invitations, written as the lord's own letter, opened in the game's plain popup. CrLetters.Deliver from a lord CR knows queues the letter with its body as given and a courier riding out today, as CR's own letters ride; the player as sender and an empty body are refused. Since 2.7.19 (tashmetu, 20/10/2026: R&S read the reply by reflection and could not tell 'set aside' from 'not read yet'): DeliverWithId gives an id, GetStatus follows it on the road, delivered, set aside (raised once through the letter window's own notice) and answered, and Replied carries the id, the sender and the reply's text, read again by GetReply. Removes the letters after.", TestLetterFromAnotherMod),
         new SelfTest("pair_regard_source", Severity.Light, "tashmetu (18/10/2026): two lords R&S made close showed as neither on the page, beside its own tooltip. A source registered through CrPairRegard, for two lords of different houses with nothing between them here, makes the first a friend of the second with its reason, leaves the other way round untouched, counts at most PairRegardPolicy.OutsideCap however much it claims, and is gone once unregistered. Since 2.7.19 the same through the function form (tashmetu, 20/10/2026: for a mod that keeps CR optional), registered and taken away by its id.", TestPairRegardSource),
         new SelfTest("npc_regard_reason", Severity.Light, "Gabriel (17/10/2026): every friend or enemy carries its reason (docs/design/NPC_REGARD.md section 9). Without touching the author's file, syncs a test betrayal of the past in which one lord wronged another, and drives the real name resolution (ContextualNameResolver.Resolve) on a message naming the betrayer: the wronged lord is told the betrayer is an enemy, for what lies between them, with the betrayal and how long ago; a lord of another house and realm asked the same is told nothing of it. The wronged lord's encyclopedia page (built here, then refreshed) lists the betrayer among his enemies and not his friends; as rows (Gabriel, 22/10/2026), the betrayer has his portrait row marked red with the betrayal as reason, and his house its banner in a red row, named through him. Then the author's file alone is synced back.", TestNpcRegardReason),
         new SelfTest("companion_news_fresh", Severity.Light, "LapitaPolata (Nexus, 17/10/2026): a companion told of a castle being besieged after it had fallen, and of a war breaking out while the player was already riding to a siege in it. Records test news and reads it through the real glue (CompanionAudienceTopicResolver.FreshnessFacts): a siege fight at a place recorded as fallen since is overtaken; a war naming the player's own realm or clan is theirs; news ten days old is too old; fresh news of two other realms may still be brought. Removes the test news.", TestCompanionNewsFresh),
